WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:07.840
Take a few seconds to appreciate this painting.

00:00:07.840 --> 00:00:13.120
What you are probably seeing right now is a&nbsp;
romantic dinner in which a man and a woman sit at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.120 --> 00:00:19.920
a table, holding hands, sharing a candlelit meal.&nbsp;
However, when we step back and see the full image,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:26.720
an enormous skull appears, occupying the entire&nbsp;
composition, staring at us from the darkness.

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:30.720
The heads of the subjects form the&nbsp;
eye sockets. The arch of the wall&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:34.960
forms the cranium, and the folds&nbsp;
of the tablecloth form the teeth.

00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:37.680
Once you see the skull, it is impossible to stop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:42.160
seeing it. And it is precisely this&nbsp;
optical illusion that has kept this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:45.440
work a mystery for more than 100 years, a&nbsp;
mystery about who could have created it.

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:50.400
This work is known as L'Amour de Pierrot,&nbsp;
which in English means Pierrot's Love.

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:56.320
If you search for it online, you will find it&nbsp;
attributed on thousands of pages to Salvador Dalí,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.320 --> 00:01:00.800
dated around 1920, when the artist&nbsp;
would have been sixteen years old.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:04.160
It is also presented as one&nbsp;
of his first masterpieces,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.160 --> 00:01:09.600
considered early proof of the genius who&nbsp;
years later would revolutionize Surrealism.

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:14.000
But there is a problem. And it is&nbsp;
that, almost with complete certainty,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:17.120
Dalí was not the artist who painted this image.

00:01:17.120 --> 00:01:20.640
When researchers have tried to&nbsp;
trace the origin of this image,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:26.080
they have realized that it has nothing to do&nbsp;
with what was believed about it for a long time.

00:01:26.080 --> 00:01:31.120
L'Amour de Pierrot is in fact not a&nbsp;
painting as such, but a postcard. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.120 --> 00:01:37.040
is a French metamorphic postcard estimated&nbsp;
to have been originally printed in 1904,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:40.240
and which began circulating around that same year.

00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:45.920
Salvador Dalí was also born in 1904, so if&nbsp;
Dalí were the true author of this image,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.920 --> 00:01:50.480
he would have had to create it as a&nbsp;
newborn, which makes no sense whatsoever.

00:01:50.480 --> 00:01:55.200
Additionally, another piece of evidence that&nbsp;
makes us doubt his authorship is that if we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.200 --> 00:02:00.480
visit Dalí's catalogue raisonné, there is&nbsp;
no official record of this painting either.

00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:06.960
But then, where did this fantastic image come&nbsp;
from? And why does everyone believe it is by Dalí?

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:10.640
The answer to the first question&nbsp;
takes us to a fascinating tradition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:14.000
that today has been almost completely forgotten.

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:17.600
At the end of the nineteenth century&nbsp;
and the beginning of the twentieth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:22.240
Europe experienced a craze for what&nbsp;
were known as metamorphic postcards.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.240 --> 00:02:26.240
These were printed images that hid&nbsp;
a second image within them. And the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:32.080
most popular of all were precisely the ones&nbsp;
that transformed everyday scenes into skulls.

00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:37.280
These postcards typically showed scenes of&nbsp;
couples in love, ladies in front of mirrors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.280 --> 00:02:42.320
or banquet scenes. But they all had something&nbsp;
in common: they were designed so that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.320 --> 00:02:46.960
when viewed from a certain distance,&nbsp;
they would turn into a human skull.

00:02:46.960 --> 00:02:52.560
These postcards were direct heirs of the&nbsp;
Baroque vanitas tradition. Those paintings from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:58.480
the sixteenth and seventeenth centuries filled&nbsp;
with skulls, clocks, and extinguished candles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.480 --> 00:03:04.000
whose purpose was to remind the viewer that&nbsp;
life is brief and that death comes for everyone.

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:09.120
Metamorphic postcards shared that same&nbsp;
message, but unlike Baroque paintings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:15.520
these images were seen more as a visual game,&nbsp;
representing pleasure and death in a single image.

00:03:15.520 --> 00:03:19.440
Something else to keep in mind is that&nbsp;
the choice of Pierrot as the protagonist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.440 --> 00:03:25.280
of this drawing is no coincidence either.&nbsp;
Pierrot was a character from Italian comedy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.280 --> 00:03:28.720
usually depicted as a sad clown with a white face,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.720 --> 00:03:34.720
and by that time he had become the universal&nbsp;
symbol of unrequited love and melancholy.

00:03:34.720 --> 00:03:40.480
With this in mind, the message behind this&nbsp;
postcard becomes much deeper. What we see is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.480 --> 00:03:45.840
not simply a couple having dinner, but a love&nbsp;
doomed from the very beginning. A love that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:51.920
without knowing it, is already forming with&nbsp;
their own bodies the image of its final destiny.

00:03:51.920 --> 00:03:55.920
And now you may be wondering, if&nbsp;
this image is an anonymous postcard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.920 --> 00:04:01.840
from 1904, why are millions of people&nbsp;
convinced that it is a work by Dalí?

00:04:01.840 --> 00:04:04.880
The answer is that the&nbsp;
confusion makes a lot of sense,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.880 --> 00:04:10.400
since Dalí built a fundamental part of his&nbsp;
career making exactly this type of image.

00:04:10.400 --> 00:04:16.080
In 1935, Dalí published an essay in which&nbsp;
he presented to the world his famous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.080 --> 00:04:22.400
paranoiac-critical method, describing it as a&nbsp;
spontaneous method of irrational knowledge, based&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.400 --> 00:04:28.480
on seeing in things what is not there. On training&nbsp;
the mind to find images within other images.

00:04:28.480 --> 00:04:32.240
And from that method some of&nbsp;
his most famous works were born.

00:04:32.240 --> 00:04:38.080
In Swans Reflecting Elephants, from 1937,&nbsp;
the swans on a lake are reflected in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.080 --> 00:04:43.520
water transformed into elephants. In&nbsp;
The Great Paranoiac, faces emerge from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.520 --> 00:04:50.720
crowds of figures. And in 1939 he painted&nbsp;
a work titled Ballerina in a Death's Head,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.720 --> 00:04:55.920
where a ballet dancer forms, with her&nbsp;
body and her dress, a human skull.

00:04:55.920 --> 00:04:58.880
Something very similar to&nbsp;
what we see in this drawing.

00:04:58.880 --> 00:05:04.800
But the most direct connection dates from&nbsp;
1951. That year, Dalí collaborated with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.800 --> 00:05:10.640
photographer Philippe Halsman on one of the most&nbsp;
famous images in the history of photography,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.640 --> 00:05:16.880
called "In Voluptate Mors." In it, seven&nbsp;
nude women pose carefully arranged to form,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.880 --> 00:05:20.400
together, an enormous skull,&nbsp;
while Dalí observes them in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.400 --> 00:05:24.960
the foreground... Exactly the same&nbsp;
concept as the old Pierrot postcard.

00:05:24.960 --> 00:05:28.080
And there lies the key to this entire confusion.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.080 --> 00:05:33.600
Although Dalí did not create L'Amour de Pierrot,&nbsp;
it is very likely that images like this one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.600 --> 00:05:38.000
which circulated throughout Europe during&nbsp;
his childhood, fed the imagination of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:43.280
boy who years later would turn the double&nbsp;
image into an entire artistic method.

00:05:43.280 --> 00:05:48.320
So it is completely wrong to claim that&nbsp;
this work was created by Salvador Dalí,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.320 --> 00:05:52.640
but there is some truth in thinking&nbsp;
that images like these were what later&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.640 --> 00:05:57.440
inspired the development of Salvador&nbsp;
Dalí's most characteristic style.

00:05:57.440 --> 00:06:01.360
But the most curious thing about all&nbsp;
of this is that L'Amour de Pierrot is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.360 --> 00:06:04.800
not the only work the internet has gifted to Dalí.

00:06:04.800 --> 00:06:08.400
You have probably seen this image&nbsp;
at some point in your life. In it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.400 --> 00:06:13.840
an elderly couple gazes tenderly at each&nbsp;
other. But as we get closer, their faces&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.840 --> 00:06:18.960
transform. The old man's face is formed by&nbsp;
a musician playing the guitar, and the old&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.960 --> 00:06:24.160
woman's by a young woman standing in a doorway.&nbsp;
They are themselves, when they were young. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.160 --> 00:06:29.760
in the center, shared between them, a golden&nbsp;
cup representing the life they built together.

00:06:29.760 --> 00:06:33.600
This image, just like L'Amour&nbsp;
de Pierrot, circulates all over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.600 --> 00:06:36.640
the internet signed as a work by Salvador Dalí.

00:06:36.640 --> 00:06:43.920
But its true author is the Mexican artist&nbsp;
Octavio Ocampo. A painter born in 1943 who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.920 --> 00:06:49.360
has dedicated his entire career to what&nbsp;
he himself calls the metamorphic style.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.360 --> 00:06:54.080
Images within images, exactly in&nbsp;
the tradition Dalí made famous.

00:06:54.080 --> 00:06:59.120
This artist is still alive today. And&nbsp;
even so, much of the world continues to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.120 --> 00:07:03.520
unfairly attribute his works to&nbsp;
an artist who died decades ago.

00:07:03.520 --> 00:07:07.920
Somehow, it seems the internet has&nbsp;
decided that every optical illusion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.920 --> 00:07:14.080
no matter who created it,&nbsp;
belongs to Salvador Dalí.

00:07:14.080 --> 00:07:17.600
As always, thank you for&nbsp;
watching the video until the end.

